<Layout title="Elements Enterprise Design System" description="A shared frontend foundation with Lit, TypeScript, framework wrappers, and an ownership model that extends from component APIs to adoption.">
  <header class="detail-hero wrap"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><div class="eyebrow">01 / Enterprise design system</div><h1>Elements.<br /><span class="serif">A foundation teams can share.</span></h1><p class="detail-deck">Bringing consistency to enterprise interfaces without asking every product team to use the same framework.</p><div class="detail-meta"><div><small>Focus</small><p>Technical ownership & frontend architecture</p></div><div><small>Foundation</small><p>Lit · TypeScript · Web Components · Nx</p></div><div><small>Across the lifecycle</small><p>APIs · Accessibility · Documentation · Adoption</p></div></div></header>
  <div class="wrap case-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#context">The challenge</a><a href="#ownership">Ownership</a><a href="#architecture">Architecture & tradeoffs</a><a href="#quality">Accessible by design</a><a href="#adoption">Releases & adoption</a><a href="#reflection">What matters</a></aside><div class="case-body">
  <section id="context"><h2>Consistency is a platform problem.</h2><p>Enterprise products need a common visual language, but their applications do not always share a framework or release cadence. Rebuilding the same interaction in each application creates more places for behavior, accessibility, and styling to drift.</p><p>Elements is the shared design-system foundation in this portfolio: a component core based on Lit and Web Components, TypeScript APIs, Angular and React wrappers, and an Nx monorepo. The goal is to make a consistent experience practical for the teams building it.</p><div class="callout">This is a generalized account of engineering scope and design considerations. Diagrams are recreated for this portfolio; internal implementations, company details, and adoption metrics are omitted.</div></section>
  <section id="ownership"><h2>Ownership beyond implementation.</h2><p>My work spans the decisions that make a component library useful as a platform: component contracts, framework integration, tokens and theming, accessibility, documentation, and the path from release to adoption.</p><ul><li><strong>Component API governance:</strong> keeping naming, properties, events, and composition patterns understandable across the system.</li><li><strong>Engineering quality:</strong> PR reviews that consider behavior, accessibility, integration, and compatibility alongside the code itself.</li><li><strong>Team enablement:</strong> documentation and mentoring that help contributors understand the reasoning behind shared patterns.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle ownership:</strong> treating releases and migrations as part of the product experience for consuming teams.</li></ul></section>
  <section id="architecture"><h2>One core. Familiar integration.</h2><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-label">Conceptual architecture / not an internal system diagram</div><div class="diagram-flow"><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Design tokens</strong><small>Theme & visual decisions</small></div><span class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Lit component core</strong><small>TypeScript · Web Components</small></div><span class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="diagram-node"><strong>Angular / React</strong><small>Wrappers → consuming apps</small></div></div><figcaption>Nx provides a shared workspace for the component core, integration packages, and supporting development workflows.</figcaption></figure><h3>Why a Web Components foundation?</h3><p>A standards-based component boundary allows the implementation to outlive a single application framework. Lit provides a focused way to build that core; TypeScript makes the public contract more explicit.</p><p>The tradeoff is integration work. Events, property bindings, forms, and framework conventions still need attention. Angular and React wrappers provide familiar entry points, while the core remains the place where shared behavior belongs.</p><h3>Tokens and theming as an API</h3><p>Semantic tokens connect design intent to implementation: what a color or spacing value means, rather than where it happens to be used. A theme should change presentation without forcing consumers to depend on private component structure.</p><h3>A shared workspace, deliberate boundaries</h3><p>An Nx monorepo makes related packages easier to develop together. The architectural discipline is to keep their boundaries clear: framework wrappers should not become independent implementations, and application-specific decisions should not quietly become system defaults.</p></section>
  <section id="quality"><h2>Accessibility belongs in the contract.</h2><p>WCAG accessibility informs component behavior as well as presentation. Keyboard operation, focus management, accessible naming, contrast, and state communication need to be considered while designing the API.</p><p>A component can support accessibility without guaranteeing that every composition is accessible. Documentation should explain both what the component provides and what the consuming application must supply.</p><h3>Review the experience, not only the markup</h3><p>My review lens includes the interaction’s full lifecycle: how users enter a component, understand its state, recover from errors, and move on. Automated checks are useful, but keyboard and assistive-technology checks remain important validation steps. This portfolio makes no blanket compliance claim.</p></section>
  <section id="adoption"><h2>A release is the start of adoption.</h2><p>A shared API is a commitment to other teams. Clear release notes, deprecation guidance, and migration examples help make changes predictable. Breaking changes deserve explicit reasoning and a practical path for consumers.</p><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram-label">Design-system lifecycle</div><p>Design intent → Component API → Implementation → Testing & accessibility → Documentation → Release → Migration & adoption</p><figcaption>A conceptual lifecycle connecting technical decisions to the teams using them.</figcaption></figure><h3>Governance through everyday work</h3><p>PR reviews and mentoring are where shared standards become habits. Explaining why a pattern exists gives contributors a basis for applying it, questioning it, and improving it without introducing a parallel system.</p></section>
  <section id="reflection"><h2>The product is developer confidence.</h2><p>The value of a design system is not the size of its component catalog. It is whether teams can understand its contracts, trust its behavior, and evolve their applications without rebuilding the same foundations.</p><p>For me, technical ownership means connecting architecture to that daily experience: the first import, the first contribution, the difficult integration, and the next migration.</p></section><a class="next-case" href="/work/ai-chatbot/"><span>Next / AI Customer Chatbot Platform</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
